{
  "answer_markdown": "I conditionally recommend evaluating [Firecrawl](https://github.com/firecrawl/firecrawl) for finding web pages and obtaining their content in Markdown/JSON; [Firecrawl MCP Server](https://github.com/firecrawl/firecrawl-mcp-server) has a separate adapter role and is needed only when a need for MCP is confirmed. The supplied README reads dated 2026-10-11 confirm these capabilities, but suitability for the project remains unknown. The catalog is a snapshot dated 2026-09-01; host reads do not update it. Firecrawl is counted once regardless of secondary categories and its former name `mendableai/firecrawl`. This removes duplicate recommendations; deduplication of retrieved documents is not confirmed.\n\nThe proposed component connection is: application → optional MCP adapter → Firecrawl → normalization and ingestion by the application. Firecrawl covers search and extraction; the team must define data contracts, deduplication, document provenance, the queue, storage and error handling. The core primarily uses AGPL-3.0, while SDKs and some UI components use MIT; the adapter's license in the snapshot is MIT. The core's cloud examples require an API key. The adapter README describes hosted, rate-limited scrape/search/parse without a key, but crawl/map/agent require a key; the absence of a key does not eliminate network access. A configurable self-hosted API URL does not prove feature equivalence between self-hosting and the cloud. For the current scenario without network access, only an isolated evaluation of contracts and the supplied sources is possible.\n\nThe single planning scenario is first validation: a hypothetical 4–8 engineer-hours, with low confidence; one engineer familiar with the target application and its component interface, at 8 hours per engineer-day. Before any integration, the workload, existing path, language/platform, chosen version and license must be confirmed. Only a synthetic check or source evaluation is proposed, without installation, credentials, a paid provider or production data. For the same scope of search and extraction, Firecrawl would require integration, dependency checks and maintenance of updates; a custom implementation would require developing and maintaining these functions; the existing or native path is unknown and requires comparison. Savings have not been established. Page rendering, extraction quality, limits, hosting and licensing affect complexity; full adoption effort and elapsed calendar time remain unknown.\n\nAll following steps have the status `proposed_not_executed`: the proposal is to check expected Markdown/JSON, source links and duplicate-handling rules on an isolated synthetic sample, then compare the same criteria with the existing path once it has been established. Such a check would not prove Firecrawl's operability or MCP activation; integration requires a separate explicit request. The preference should be reconsidered if the required capabilities are unavailable in an acceptable configuration, the license is not accepted or the existing path meets the same criteria at lower cost. Because the current path is unknown, the trial should remain isolated, without changing authoritative data or the queue; abandoning it preserves the original state. The decisive question is: do you need to discover new URLs through search, or is extracting content from predefined URLs sufficient?",
